   Relevance: Directly the chapter requested; the AI “Key takeaways” section on 
the page exposes basic physical measurements and frames them in the authenticity
discussion.  
   Excerpts / faithful summary:  
   - The Academia page provides an abstract stating that the paper documents the
authenticity of the so‑called Ring of Nestor (CMS VI, no. 277), reassessing 
earlier tendencies to classify certain Creto‑Mycenaean gold rings (including 
those of Nestor and Minos) as fakes and arguing instead for their authenticity 
on the basis of new comparanda and technical study.  
   - The abstract emphasizes that recent finds with comparable iconography (wall
paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories, etc.) and closer study of techniques 
and craftsmanship of early Late Bronze Age signet rings (16th–15th c. BC) have 
“significantly reinforced” the revision in favor of authenticity.  
   - The “Key takeaways” AI section on the same page (clearly marked as such) 
states that “The Ring of Nestor, measuring 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, is the fifth 
largest Aegean signet ring,” explicitly giving the two dimensions and ranking 
the ring by size among Aegean signet rings. (The snippet does not specify 
whether these measurements refer to the bezel/engraved field, overall plate, or 
include the hoop.)  
   - The “Key takeaways” also summarize that the paper confirms the ring’s 
authenticity, counters earlier claims of forgery from the 1920s, identifies the 
so‑called “watch-dog” or “dragon” figure as a scorpion (an apotropaic Bronze Age
symbol), and relates this iconographic reinterpretation to the wider argument 
about the ring’s meaning and authenticity.

