"citations": [13]}, {"id": "20260628170409745803_2cb57d46", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The Academia.edu abstract for 'The Lord of the Gold Rings' notes: 'Both
the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos have bands strikingly similar ... 
Thisbe, Boeotia, JHS 45, pp. 1–75. Furtwängler, A. 1900. Die antiken ...', 
explicitly citing Evans’s JHS 45 article on Thisbe, Boeotia and referencing 
Furtwängler’s 1900 work.", "source_ids": ["20260628170407069051_c386447d"], 
"citations": [14]}, {"id": "20260628170409835461_167de393", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The abstract explains that the Ring of Nestor’s band is 'strikingly 
similar' to that of the Ring of Minos, using band morphology as comparative data
in a broader analysis of Aegean gold rings.", "source_ids": 
["20260628170407069051_c386447d"], "citations": [14]}, {"id": 
"20260628170409919797_7e79addc", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The summary of 
'The Lord of the Gold Rings' states that the article 
'利用戒圈形制、尺寸和工艺作为年代和工坊归属的线索，进一步确认其大致落入晚米诺斯
／迈锡尼精英葬具传统' (uses band form, dimensions, and craftsmanship as clues to
dating and workshop attribution, further confirming that the ring broadly falls 
within a Late Minoan/Mycenaean elite funerary tradition).", "source_ids": 
["20260628170407069051_c386447d"], "citations": [14]}, {"id": 
"20260628170438979838_d6894683", "type": "Claim", "text": "According to Arthur 
Evans’s 1925 Journal of Hellenic Studies article, the object now known as the 
Ring of Nestor was part of a sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead 
seals discovered in a tomb at Thisbê (Thisbe), Boeotia, and has since served as 
the primary published record of the ring’s grave context and associated 
assemblage.", "source_ids": ["20260628165852403005_c0352755", 
"20260628170407069051_c386447d"], "citations": [2, 14]}, {"id": 
"20260628170439210333_ce7b7b0d", "type": "Claim", "text": "Early and 
mid‑20th‑century scholarship, drawing on Evans and related typological work, 
generally treated the Ring of Nestor from Thisbê as a genuine Late Minoan or 
Mycenaean high‑status funerary object within an Aegean elite burial tradition, 
but later studies introduced doubts that it might be a forgery and re‑examined 
its authenticity.", "source_ids": ["20260628165852403005_c0352755", 
"20260628170043086880_97dad9bd", "20260628170407069051_c386447d"], "citations": 
[2, 3, 14]}, {"id": "20260628170439469226_b1a33edf", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Recent technical and iconographic studies have used manufacturing techniques, 
goldworking details, and iconographic comparison with authenticated Minoan and 
Mycenaean rings and seals to reassess the Ring of Nestor’s date, provenance, and
authenticity; one technical study reports new observations that are presented as
supporting the ring’s authenticity and as evidence for a Late Minoan/Mycenaean 
cultural attribution.", "source_ids": ["20260628170043086880_97dad9bd", 
"20260628170403694581_fe8ad1ab"], "citations": [3, 12]}, {"id": 
"20260628170439749740_a64a6e5e", "type": "Claim", "text": "Modern comparative 
studies such as 'The Lord of the Gold Rings' use the band form, dimensions, and 
craftsmanship of the Ring of Nestor—particularly its striking similarity to the 
Ring of Minos—as typological evidence for placing it among Late Minoan/Mycenaean
elite funerary gold rings and for discussing its possible workshop 
attribution.", "source_ids": ["20260628170407069051_c386447d"], "citations": 
[14]}, {"id": "20260628170439921097_b08ead1c", "type": "Claim", "text": "At 
least one Hellenic Studies publication discusses the griffin motif on the Ring 
of Nestor within a section on 'Gold intaglios, Minoan', implicitly treating its 
imagery as comparable to Minoan gold intaglio art, though the publication’s 
explicit dating or cultural attribution for the ring is not clear from the 
available excerpts.", "source_ids": ["20260628170407034954_3c37768b"], 
"citations": [13]}, {"id": "20260628170440036020_8439e6cb", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "The provenance and collection history of the Ring of Nestor are 
partially documented in scholarship through Evans’s 1925 publication and later 
studies that trace its scholarly reception and note that the ring was withdrawn 
from display and then placed back on exhibition in 1995, but the available 
material does not specify the museum, accession number, or a continuous chain of
custody from discovery to its current collection.", "source_ids": 
["20260628165852403005_c0352755", "20260628170403694581_fe8ad1ab"], "citations":
[2, 12]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 40, "total": 19, "next_offset": null}
